     "text": "```json\n{\"count\": 0, \"rationale\": \"Every step is reachable, and the target has several independent routes. Route A: kerberoast:credentialAccess (from the wifiGuest or vpnGateway footholds) leads to backupHost:lateralMovement, then codeRepo:collection, then crownData:exfiltrate. backupHost also has a second way in through workstation:execution and browserStore:credentialAccess. Route B: mailClient:execution leads to tokenCache:credentialAccess; that step plus browserStore gives dbLogin:authenticate, which leads to printServer:lateralMovement (also reachable through rdpLogin with memoryDump), then shareMount:collection, then the target. Route C: dbLogin:authenticate leads to jumpHost:lateralMovement, then dbReplica:collection, then the target. Blocking any single step leaves at least one of these routes open. Blocking backupHost leaves Routes B and C. Blocking tokenCache, printServer or dbLogin leaves Route A. Blocking codeRepo or kerberoast leaves Routes B and C, and backupHost stays reachable through browserStore. Blocking workstation leaves kerberoast to backupHost to codeRepo. So no step is a single point of failure.\"}\n```"
    }
